    "result_text": "All 283 tests pass, including the new `Cp17Tests`. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `sharesHousehold` flag on owner creation. Because the test does `fetchOwner(createOwnerOk(...))` \u2014 creating via `POST` then reading the value back via a fresh `GET` \u2014 the value has to be **computed at creation time and persisted**, not just placed on the POST response.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `sharesHousehold` boolean to the `OwnerDto` schema, so the generated DTO carries the field and MapStruct maps it by name.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `Boolean sharesHousehold` field (`@Column(name = \"shares_household\")`) with getter/setter, mirroring the existing computed fields (`namesakeCount`, `membershipTier`).\n\n3. **Schemas** (hsqldb, h2, postgres, mysql) \u2014 added the `shares_household` column so the persisted value survives to the follow-up `GET`.\n\n4. **`AssignSharesHousehold.java`** \u2014 new OfficeFloor function step that, before the owner is saved, sets the flag to `true` when any existing owner already has the same address and city, else `false`. It runs after `NormalizeCity`, so city comparisons use each city's canonical spelling.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `assignSharesHousehold` into the pipeline between `assignNamesakeCount` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\nThe first owner at a new address gets `false`; a second owner at the same address + city gets `true`, and creation still succeeds.",
